{
  "teamId": 141,
  "year": 2022,
  "narrative": "The 2022 Toronto Blue Jays arrived with legitimate expectations, and they largely delivered, finishing 92-70 and punching a Wild Card ticket. April set the tone with a mix of grit and fragility: Matt Chapman announced himself as the key offseason addition with a clutch extra-base hit to win a game, but closer Jordan Romano suffered a brutal blown save when Jeremy Pena took him deep against Houston, a sign that late-game drama would shadow this team all year long. Still, the Jays finished April 14-8, one of the better months any AL team would post.\n\nMay and June offered a rockier ride. Aaron Judge's three-run blast off Romano in May was a gut punch in a rivalry matchup, and a Paul Goldschmidt grand slam stung in St. Louis. The rotation and bullpen showed cracks, but the offense answered repeatedly — Chapman delivered another critical double to beat the Angels, and Teoscar Hernández launched a three-run shot in June to keep the team afloat. Through all the turbulence, Toronto stayed above .500, going 14-12 in May and 15-13 in June, grinding through adversity more than dominating anyone.\n\nJuly and August brought genuine concern. Romano continued to give up costly home runs, the bullpen leaked, and the lineup went cold at key moments — the team finished July at 14-12 and slipped below .500 in August at 13-14. But September was the salvation. Bo Bichette turned in one of his best months, including a towering two-run blast on September 12th that stands as the single most valuable positive play of the second half. George Springer added a three-run shot days later, and Vladimir Guerrero Jr. delivered a late-inning single in a pennant-race game against the Yankees. The Jays went 18-10 in September, clinching a Wild Card spot and entering October with momentum.\n\nThe Wild Card series against the Seattle Mariners became the season's crushing final chapter. Alek Manoah was nicked for a Cal Raleigh home run in Game 1, and though Hernández responded with a two-run shot in Game 2 to keep hope alive, Romano could not hold the fort. J.P. Crawford's two-run double and Adam Frazier's RBI double in the critical inning unraveled the closer and the Blue Jays' season alike. Toronto was eliminated in the Wild Card round, leaving a talented, young core with a winter to wonder what might have been — and a 92-win season that felt, in the end, like unfinished business.",
  "chapters": [
